  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nThis Afternoon and Tonight:\n\nThe Northland is in a bit of a lull, storm wise, this afternoon.\nWidespread cloud cover has kept temperatures in the 70s over the\nArrowhead while elsewhere, temps are in the low to mid 80s. A \nstalled front is sitting just to our west, with more widespread \nshowers and a couple storms remaining back over the Red River \nValley. Through the rest of the afternoon, south winds will \ncontinue to increase, bringing warm air and moisture northward. \nThis morning's INL sounding showed a substantial inversion (and \na cap around 800mb). If there are enough breaks in the clouds to\nbuild more instability, an area of 50-60kts of bulk shear is \nnosing into the Lake of the Woods and Kooch county. If a storm \ncan develop this afternoon, it could be strong to severe. If it \ncan become surface based, there is a non-zero chance of a \ntornado. But, forcing looks weak, as we wait for the next\nshortwave to kick the front eastward. \n\nThe main impact period will be this evening into early Tuesday\nmorning, as a broken line of storms along an approaching cold \nfront strengthens to our west and moves into northeast MN. The \nairmass ahead of the front will be characterized by 1000-1500 \nJ/kg (MUCAPE), 40-60kt of bulk shear right along the front, and \nlapse rates of at least 7C/km. Storms could be strong to severe,\nwith large hail and damaging winds possible. Cannot rule out a \ntornado within the broken line. \n\nStorms will continue to move across the region through the \novernight period with the severe risk fading in the early \nmorning hours as instability and wind features weaken. \nScattered showers could linger across portions of northwest WI \nand the tip of the Arrowhead through mid morning tomorrow. \n\nMid week:\n\nSurface high pressure moves over the region for for mid week as\nthe upper level pattern supports broad troughing across much of\nthe northern CONUS. Drier air will likely keep conditions dry\ndespite the troughing aloft. A stronger shortwave arrives this\nweekend, bringing better rain chances. Temperatures stay in the\n70s through Wednesday, then slowly climb through the end of the\nwork week.",
